arXiv:2602.20528v1 [cs.CL] 24 Feb 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Justin Lovelace
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span> Corresponding author: <a href="mailto:jl3353@cornell.edu" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">jl3353@cornell.edu</a></span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Christian Belardi
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sofian Zalouk
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Adhitya Polavaram
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Srivatsa Kundurthy,  Kilian Q Weinberger</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science, Cornell University
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The Stop-Think-AutoRegress Language Diffusion Model (STAR-LDM) integrates latent diffusion planning with autoregressive generation. Unlike conventional autoregressive language models limited to token-by-token decisions, STAR-LDM incorporates a “thinking” phase that pauses generation to refine a semantic plan through diffusion before continuing. This enables global planning in continuous space prior to committing to discrete tokens. Evaluations show STAR-LDM significantly outperforms similar-sized models on language understanding benchmarks and achieves <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;70\%</annotation></semantics></math> win rates in LLM-as-judge comparisons for narrative coherence and commonsense reasoning. The architecture also allows straightforward control through lightweight classifiers, enabling fine-grained steering of attributes without model retraining while maintaining better fluency-control trade-offs than specialized approaches. Our code is available at <a href="https://github.com/justinlovelace/STAR-LDM" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/justinlovelace/STAR-LDM</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs), typically based on the Transformer architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across a vast range of natural language tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Their success largely stems from the autoregressive (AR) generation paradigm, where text is produced sequentially, predicting one token at a time conditioned on the preceding context.
However, this strictly left-to-right, token-by-token generation process fundamentally differs from human writing practices. Human authors frequently pause, reflect, plan ahead, and revise their text to ensure global coherence, stylistic consistency, and adherence to overarching goals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hayes and Flower (1996)</a></cite>. In contrast, standard autoregressive models make irrevocable, local decisions at each step, which inherently limits their capacity for long-range planning or dynamic control over the generation process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021b</a>)</cite>. Consequently, ensuring that generated text globally satisfies complex constraints, maintains a consistent persona, or avoids undesirable content purely through local token predictions remains a significant challenge, often necessitating computationally expensive fine-tuning or reinforcement learning procedures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziegler et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in generative modeling, particularly score-based diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, offer a fundamentally different paradigm. These models generate data through an iterative denoising process, beginning with pure noise and guided by a learned score function. Crucially, this score function can be readily modified during generation to incorporate external guidance signals from lightweight classifiers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Salimans, 2021</a>)</cite>, enabling flexible control over various attributes of the generated output (e.g., style, content). This inherent controllability, which can be effectively applied in continuous spaces such as image latents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2021</a>)</cite> or text embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lovelace et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, presents a particularly promising direction for enhancing control mechanisms in large language models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by the planning capabilities suggested by human writing and the controllability of diffusion models, we propose <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stop-Think-AutoRegress Language Diffusion Model (STAR-LDM)</span>. <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">STAR-LDM</span> is a novel, unified architecture that integrates latent diffusion planning directly into the autoregressive generation framework. It introduces a “thinking” phase where standard token generation is paused. During this phase, the model utilizes a diffusion process operating in a continuous sentence embedding space to refine a latent representation (a soft prompt) that encodes a plan for the subsequent text. This refined latent representation then guides the resumption of autoregressive token generation. By jointly training the autoregressive and diffusion components, STAR-LDM learns to perform global semantic planning in a continuous space <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">before</span> committing to discrete token choices, enabling more coherent and controllable generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are as follows:
(1) We propose STAR-LDM, a unified architecture that integrates latent diffusion planning within an autoregressive language model.
(2) We demonstrate that STAR-LDM achieves improved language generation quality.
(3) We show that our unified architecture leads to substantial improvements on standard language understanding benchmarks, indicating that the planning mechanism enhances, rather than hinders, core language capabilities.
(4) We validate that STAR-LDM’s integrated diffusion component enables efficient, plug-and-play control over text attributes (like sentiment and toxicity) using lightweight classifiers during inference, without requiring model retraining.
STAR-LDM represents a step towards language models that can ”stop and think” entirely in latent space, leading to higher-quality, more controllable, and potentially more reliable text generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma and Gao, 2023</a>)</cite>, are a class of generative models that iteratively refine a sample of noise to a sample from some data distribution in a coarse-to-fine manner. Given clean data <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}})</annotation></semantics></math>, diffusion models learn a model <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}})</annotation></semantics></math> approximating <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> could be images, audio, or continuous latent representations relevant to NLP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lovelace et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, such as the sentence embeddings used in this work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Forward process.</span> Diffusion models define a forward noising process that transitions clean data <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> to noise over continuous time <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. The noisy latent <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{z}_{t};\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}},\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{z}_{t};\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}},\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be sampled as:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}=\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}+\sigma_{t}\bm{\epsilon},\quad\text{where}\quad\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}=\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}+\sigma_{t}\bm{\epsilon},\quad\text{where}\quad\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Using the variance-preserving formulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite> (<math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}=1-\alpha_{t}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}=1-\alpha_{t}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>), the noise level is parameterized by the log Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR), <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lambda_{t}=\log(\alpha_{t}^{2}/\sigma_{t}^{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lambda_{t}=\log(\alpha_{t}^{2}/\sigma_{t}^{2})}</annotation></semantics></math>. The schedule <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases such that <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{0}\approx\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{0}\approx\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the distribution of <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> approaches a standard normal, i.e., <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}})\approx\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}})\approx\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generative model.</span> The generative process reverses the forward process, starting from noise <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> and iteratively denoising it to produce a final sample <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> that approximates the data distribution <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This requires the score function <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{z}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{z}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, the gradient of the log-density of noisy data at time <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Diffusion models introduce a neural network <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate this score. Sampling involves iteratively applying the learned model <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (or an equivalent parameterization, like noise prediction) to reverse the diffusion from <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, often using solvers like DDPM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training objective.</span> The score network <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> can be learned via denoising score matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Ermon, 2019</a>)</cite>. An equivalent and common objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite> trains a network <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> to predict the noise <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> added to <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> to get <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, using a loss like:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t,\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}\sim q(\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}),\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})}\left[w(\lambda_{t})\cdot\|\bm{\epsilon}-\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)\|_{2}^{2}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t,\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}\sim q(\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}),\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})}\left[w(\lambda_{t})\cdot\|\bm{\epsilon}-\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)\|_{2}^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}=\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}+\sigma_{t}\bm{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}=\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{\text{data}}+\sigma_{t}\bm{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\lambda_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\lambda_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is an SNR-dependent weighting term <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. The score and noise predictions are related by <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)=-\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)/\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)=-\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)/\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Plug-and-play control.</span>
A key advantage of diffusion, as opposed to autoregressive, models is their controllability via plug-and-play guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021</a>)</cite>. To generate samples conditioned on <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, the sampling process is guided by the conditional score <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Bayes’ rule, this conditional score can be decomposed:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\mathbf{y})=\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{z}_{t})}_{\text{Unconditional Score}}+\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z}_{t})}_{\text{Guidance Term}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Unconditional Score</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Guidance Term</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\mathbf{y})=\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{z}_{t})}_{\text{Unconditional Score}}+\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z}_{t})}_{\text{Guidance Term}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The first term is the standard score approximated by the unconditional diffusion model <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>. The second term guides the sampling towards latents <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that are likely to produce the condition <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. This guidance term can be approximated using gradients from a <span id="S2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">separate classifier</span> trained on noisy data, which is often called <span id="S2.p5.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">classifier guidance</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, a guidance scale <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is introduced to modulate the strength of the condition:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{s}}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\mathbf{y})=\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{z}_{t})+s\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z}_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{s}}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\mathbf{y})=\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{z}_{t})+s\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Setting <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math> yields unconditional generation, while <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\!&gt;\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\!&gt;\!1</annotation></semantics></math> increases the influence of <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. This provides flexible “plug-and-play” control without retraining the base model for each condition <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. This inherent controllability offers a promising avenue to address the control challenges of autoregressive language generation, which motivates our work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Stop-Think-AutoRegress Language Diffusion Model</h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20528v1/architecture_overview.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/177;" width="476" height="177" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Unified diffusion-guided language model architecture illustrating the training (left) and generation (right) processes. See text for details.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stop-Think-AutoRegress Language Diffusion Model (STAR-LDM)</span>, a unified architecture that augments autoregressive language modeling with latent diffusion planning. STAR-LDM can pause generation, plan in a continuous latent space, and resume autoregressive token generation with improved coherence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Stop-Think-AutoRegress Language Diffusion Model ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> depicts our proposed architecture, which consists of three major components, each with a primary role in enabling our ”stop-think-autoregress” approach:
(1) The <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#A07BC8;">Autoregressive Decoder</span> (in purple) serves as the foundation of our model, handling token generation in a sequential manner similar to traditional language models. This component maintains the local fluency and grammatical structure necessary for coherent text.
(2) The continuous <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#5A90C0;">Sentence Embedding Space</span> (blue vectors) provides a latent planning space that captures semantic meaning. Using 768-dimensional Sentence-T5 XL embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, this space represents text at a higher level of abstraction than tokens, with representations that capture core semantic content while being invariant to shallow surface variations.
(3) Two <span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#7DBB7D;">Diffusion Transformers (DiTs)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peebles and Xie, 2022</a>)</cite> (in green) facilitate the translation between the continuous embedding space and the discrete token representations used by the decoder.
These transformer encoders are also conditioned on the noise level through adaptive normalization layers following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peebles and Xie (2022)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our approach, text generation is conceptualized as a two-part process: first processing a <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prefix</span> (the initial portion of text that has already been generated), then planning and generating a coherent <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">continuation</span>. The prefix might be a prompt provided by a user or previously generated text, while the continuation represents new text the model will generate with guidance from a semantic plan.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">During training, these components work together as follows.
First, the autoregressive decoder processes the prefix text <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}}</annotation></semantics></math> (“<span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">The old clock ticked</span>”) like a standard autoregressive language model. The model also processes a noisy representation of the continuation’s semantic meaning. This representation is created by encoding the ground truth continuation <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{cont}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>cont</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{cont}}</annotation></semantics></math> (“<span id="S3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">relentlessly as...</span>”) into a clean embedding <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> using Sentence-T5, then adding noise according to the diffusion schedule to obtain <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}=\alpha_{t}\mathbf{z}_{\text{data}}+\sigma_{t}\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}=\alpha_{t}\mathbf{z}_{\text{data}}+\sigma_{t}\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We linearly project this noisy embedding into a sequence of 8 soft vectors, which the first DiT processes (conditioning on the current noise level) to form a soft prompt. This prompt will both be used to guide diffusion denoising and subsequent token generation. For diffusion denoising, the autoregressive decoder naturally incorporates information from the prefix as it processes this soft prompt.
The decoder’s outputs then flow to the second DiT, which generates the final representations for the diffusion prediction. We apply a learned linear projection to obtain the final diffusion prediction <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, completing a step of the denoising process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">After processing this soft prompt, the decoder reverts to standard next-token prediction. The predictions for the text continuation are therefore conditioned on both the textual prefix and the (noisy) sentence embedding of that text continuation. The sentence embedding provides a valuable signal for the token prediction objective, teaching the decoder to generate text aligned with the sentence embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This integrated design creates a model that can simultaneously handle token-level prediction and semantic-level planning. As noise levels decrease during training or sampling, the semantic guidance becomes increasingly precise, enabling the model to generate continuations that follow the semantic content in the denoised embedding while maintaining both local fluency and global coherence.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Training Procedure</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our training approach combines autoregressive language modeling with diffusion-based planning in a multi-task learning framework. This training strategy will enable STAR-LDM to learn both token generation and semantic planning simultaneously.
During self-supervised pre-training (left side of <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Stop-Think-AutoRegress Language Diffusion Model ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a>), we sample text segments and randomly split each into a prefix <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and continuation <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{cont}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>cont</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{cont}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This simulates stopping points where the model would pause to plan. We jointly optimize with two objectives:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Language Modeling Loss (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">LM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span> The loss combines standard prefix modeling and noise-conditioned continuation modeling:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}=\underbrace{-\sum_{i=1}^{p}\log P(x_{\text{pref},i}|\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref},&lt;i};\theta)}_{\text{Prefix modeling}}-\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{n-p}\log P(x_{\text{cont},j}|\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}},\mathbf{x}_{\text{cont},&lt;j},\mathbf{z}_{t};\theta)}_{\text{Continuation modeling}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>LM</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>pref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>pref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Prefix modeling</mtext></munder><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mtext>cont</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mrow><mtext>cont</mtext><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Continuation modeling</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}=\underbrace{-\sum_{i=1}^{p}\log P(x_{\text{pref},i}|\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref},&lt;i};\theta)}_{\text{Prefix modeling}}-\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{n-p}\log P(x_{\text{cont},j}|\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}},\mathbf{x}_{\text{cont},&lt;j},\mathbf{z}_{t};\theta)}_{\text{Continuation modeling}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is the prefix length, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> the total length, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the noisy embedding of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{cont}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>cont</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{cont}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion Loss (<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">DM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DM}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span> We encode the continuation <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{cont}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>cont</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{cont}}</annotation></semantics></math> using Sentence-T5 to obtain the clean embedding <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Following standard diffusion training, we then sample a timestep <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and noise vector <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>, compute the noisy embedding <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}=\alpha_{t}\mathbf{z}_{0}+\sigma_{t}\bm{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}=\alpha_{t}\mathbf{z}_{0}+\sigma_{t}\bm{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, and train the model to predict the added noise conditioned on the prefix and timestep:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DM}}=\mathbb{E}_{t,\mathbf{x}_{\text{cont}},\bm{\epsilon}}\left[w(\lambda_{t})\cdot\|\bm{\epsilon}-\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}})\|_{2}^{2}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DM</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>cont</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DM}}=\mathbb{E}_{t,\mathbf{x}_{\text{cont}},\bm{\epsilon}}\left[w(\lambda_{t})\cdot\|\bm{\epsilon}-\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}})\|_{2}^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This objective is weighted by <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\lambda_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\lambda_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, a function of the log(SNR) that balances the importance of different noise levels<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            We adopt the sigmoid weighting and cosine noise schedule used by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoogeboom et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</span></span></span>. The final training objective combines both losses, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}+\beta\mathcal{L}_{\text{DM}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>LM</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DM</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}+\beta\mathcal{L}_{\text{DM}}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where the scalar <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> balances the two components. STAR-LDM is therefore trained for autoregression and diffusion in a multi-task manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Noise Conditioning.</span> The diffusion training process exposes the autoregressive decoder to embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with varying noise levels, from <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math> (pure noise) to <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math> (clean). Like prior work that has utilized noise-conditioning to improve robustness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lovelace et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, our decoder learns to adapt based on noise level: relying heavily on the semantic plan at low noise while falling back to standard prefix conditioning at high noise. This creates a natural control parameter during inference—the noise level—that adjusts the balance between diffusion planning and autoregressive generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Generation Process</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our training process optimizes STAR-LDM to both generate coherent text and denoise semantic embeddings, the inference process leverages these capabilities in a sequential ”Stop-Think-AutoRegress” workflow. During inference, we assume a prompted setting where the user provides a prefix, and the model plans and generates a continuation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During inference (<a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Stop-Think-AutoRegress Language Diffusion Model ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a>, right), our approach unfolds in three distinct phases:
1) <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stop</span>: The model processes the input prompt <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}}</annotation></semantics></math> (provided by the user) through the autoregressive decoder.
2) <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Think</span>: The model then “thinks” by sampling initial noise <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{1}=\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{1}=\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> and performing iterative denoising to obtain a semantic plan. This iterative denoising process, guided by the prefix <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, gradually transforms random noise into a coherent semantic embedding, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Starting from pure noise <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model applies its trained noise prediction network <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, consisting of a pass through the network, over multiple timesteps (e.g. 50 steps). At each step <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the model uses the predicted noise component to reduce the noise level of the embedding following a predetermined schedule. This process iteratively refines a coherent plan consistent with the given prefix. The generation process concludes with the third phase: 3) AutoRegress. Here, the generated semantic plan <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> guides the autoregressive decoder to produce a fluent continuation <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{\text{cont}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>cont</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{\text{cont}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is achieved by processing <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> through the first DiT to create a soft prompt that conditions the decoder alongside <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Once conditioned on this combined prompt, the decoder generates the continuation via standard autoregressive sampling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt guidance.</span>
During the thinking phase, we can optionally strengthen the influence of the prefix on the generated plan using classifier-free guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Salimans, 2021</a>)</cite>. This technique combines an unconditional prediction (ignoring the prefix) with a conditional prediction (using the prefix) to amplify the prefix’s influence:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{\epsilon}}_{t}=\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\emptyset)+w\cdot(\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}})-\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\emptyset))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{\epsilon}}_{t}=\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\emptyset)+w\cdot(\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\mathbf{x}_{\text{pref}})-\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\emptyset))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is the guidance scale and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\emptyset)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\emptyset)</annotation></semantics></math> represents an unconditional prediction obtained by masking the prefix embeddings from the diffusion model. Higher values of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> increase the prompt’s influence on the semantic plan.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Plug-and-Play Control with Classifier Guidance.</span>
To enable efficient classifier guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021</a>)</cite>, we train an MLP that directly operates on noisy sentence embeddings:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z}_{t})\approx f_{\phi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)=\text{MLP}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\text{TimeEmb}(t))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>MLP</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>TimeEmb</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z}_{t})\approx f_{\phi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)=\text{MLP}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\text{TimeEmb}(t)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{TimeEmb}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>TimeEmb</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{TimeEmb}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the noise level via sinusoidal embeddings. To make the MLP robust to varying noise levels, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol (2021)</a></cite> and train on labeled data that we perturb with randomly chosen noise levels.
During inference, we compute the guidance gradient directly:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z}_{t})\approx\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}f_{\phi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z}_{t})\approx\nabla_{\mathbf{z}_{t}}f_{\phi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">This lightweight approach enables attribute control with negligible computational overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span>
We implement our model using GPT2-Large as the autoregressive backbone which has 770M parameters. Our unified architecture, with the two small DiT encoders, has 956M trainable parameters. During training, we use the sigmoid loss weighting and cosine noise schedule following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoogeboom et al. (2024)</a></cite>. For diffusion generation, we use a cosine noise schedule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021</a>)</cite> with 50 diffusion steps and add small noise (<math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>) to the final generated embedding <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure robustness to minor errors introduced by diffusion sampling. We train our model on approximately 16B tokens from the FineWeb dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Penedo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We report full architecture details and hyperparameters in <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Implementation Details ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Impact of Diffusion Process on Language Modeling</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next token prediction in traditional autoregressive models focuses primarily on local coherence, making these models inherently myopic in their generation decisions. In contrast, our diffusion-based planning approach introduces a form of non-local semantic guidance. To understand how this semantic guidance influences token prediction, we conducted a controlled experiment visualized in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Impact of Diffusion Process on Language Modeling ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
We begin with a simple prefix (”I went to the carnival and”) and two distinct continuations describing different experiences. For each continuation, we compute token probabilities under two conditions: (1) when conditioned on a clean semantic embedding of the entire continuation (<math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>) and (2) when conditioned on pure Gaussian noise (<math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>). The difference in log likelihoods, <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\log p(x_{cont}|x_{pref},z_{0})-\log p(x_{cont}|x_{pref},\epsilon)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\log p(x_{cont}|x_{pref},z_{0})-\log p(x_{cont}|x_{pref},\epsilon)}</annotation></semantics></math>, reveals which tokens are most influenced by semantic planning with the ground-truth embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Impact of Diffusion Process on Language Modeling ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the semantic plan primarily increases the likelihood of content-bearing words that capture the essence of each narrative. In the first continuation, words like ”ring toss,” ”gold fish,” ”named,” and ”Flash” show the largest positive shifts. These precisely correspond to the key narrative elements describing winning a pet fish at a carnival game. Similarly, in the second continuation, words like ”lost,” ”mirrors,” ”eaten,” and ”funnel” experience the greatest positive influence—all central to a different carnival experience involving a hall of mirrors and food.
This analysis demonstrates that our model’s diffusion-based planning mechanism effectively captures and emphasizes semantically meaningful content, rather than merely influencing surface-level linguistic patterns. By explicitly integrating a latent semantic plan, STAR-LDM generates coherent language that maintains global semantic consistency. We present additional experiments exploring this semantic influence across varying noise levels in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Additional Visualizations of the Impact of Diffusion Process on Language Modeling ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20528v1/delta_loglikelihood_heatmap.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/155;" width="476" height="155" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Visualization of the change in log likelihood due to conditioning on the clean continuation embedding versus pure noise. Tokens highlighted in red represent the information provided by the semantic plan.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Natural Language Understanding</h2>

<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20528v1/nlu_eval_diagram.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/155;" width="476" height="155" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Diagram comparing zero-shot NLU evaluation for standard LMs (left) and STAR-LDM (right). Both models score candidate answers (<math id="S5.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) given a question (<math id="S5.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>). STAR-LDM utilizes both the answer text <math id="S5.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and its latent embedding <math id="S5.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i,0}</annotation></semantics></math> from Sentence-T5 for scoring.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Standard Natural Language Understanding (NLU) benchmarks, particularly multiple-choice question answering (QA) datasets (e.g., CSQA, SIQA, ARC), assess a model’s ability to identify the most plausible answer or completion (<math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>) from a set of candidates, given a context or question (<math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>). Conventional autoregressive models ( <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5 Natural Language Understanding ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a>, Left Panel) typically score each candidate answer <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by its negative log-likelihood: <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}(A_{i}|Q)=-\log p(A_{i}|Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>LM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}(A_{i}|Q)=-\log p(A_{i}|Q)</annotation></semantics></math>. This likelihood is calculated using teacher forcing, and the candidate with the lowest score (highest probability) is selected as the answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We adapt this protocol for STAR-LDM to select the most likely candidate answer given the question <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5 Natural Language Understanding ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a>, Right Panel). We assess candidate answers by approximating <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\log p(A_{i}|Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\log p(A_{i}|Q)</annotation></semantics></math> using the variational lower bound on the likelihood <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, as direct calculation is intractable.
We encode each candidate <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> using Sentence T5 to produce a semantic latent plan <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i,0}</annotation></semantics></math>, add Gaussian noise to generate <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> at a specific timestep <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and compute our scoring function derived from the Evidence Lower Bound (ELBO):</p>
<table id="S5.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\log p(A_{i}|Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\log p(A_{i}|Q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}(A_{i}|\mathbf{z}_{i,0},Q)+\mathcal{L}_{\text{DM}}(\mathbf{z}_{i,t}|Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>LM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}(A_{i}|\mathbf{z}_{i,0},Q)+\mathcal{L}_{\text{DM}}(\mathbf{z}_{i,t}|Q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sum_{j=1}^{|A_{i}|}\log p(A_{i}^{j}|A_{i}^{&lt;j},\mathbf{z}_{i,0},Q)+\mathbb{E}_{t,\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)}[w_{\text{ELBO}}(\lambda_{t})\cdot||\epsilon-\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{i,t},t,Q)||^{2}_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mtext>ELBO</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sum_{j=1}^{|A_{i}|}\log p(A_{i}^{j}|A_{i}^{&lt;j},\mathbf{z}_{i,0},Q)+\mathbb{E}_{t,\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)}[w_{\text{ELBO}}(\lambda_{t})\cdot||\epsilon-\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{i,t},t,Q)||^{2}_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the weighting <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\text{ELBO}}(\lambda_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mtext>ELBO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\text{ELBO}}(\lambda_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for the regression objective follows from the derivation of the lower bound <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. We present a detailed derivation of this scoring function in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C NLU Derivation ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix C</span></a>.
The LM term measures how well the model reconstructs <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i,0}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the diffusion term measures how plausible the latent plan <math id="S5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i,0}</annotation></semantics></math> is for question <math id="S5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. We select the candidate with the minimum overall score, choosing the answer that is both linguistically well-formed and has a plausible semantic meaning. We compute the expectation in  <a href="#S5.E10" title="10 ‣ 5 Natural Language Understanding ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation 10</span></a> with an unbiased Monte Carlo estimate of the weighted reconstruction error.
In practice, autoregressive models often normalize the log-likelihood, <math id="S5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>LM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}</annotation></semantics></math>, by the length of the candidate <math id="S5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to improve performance. We also adopt this approach as we observe it similarly improves performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
STAR-LDM outperforms similarly sized autoregressive models on standard zero-shot NLU benchmarks<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          The benchmarks include CommonsenseQA (CSQA), Social IQA (SIQA), HellaSwag (HS), Winogrande (WG), Physical IQA (PIQA), OpenBookQA (OBQA), and ARC (Easy and Challenge).</span></span></span>, as shown in <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5 Natural Language Understanding ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a>. The model excels particularly on commonsense reasoning tasks (CSQA, SIQA), demonstrating the effectiveness of latent semantic planning. Notably, the diffusion component alone performs strongly, indicating that semantic plausibility contributes significantly to reasoning capabilities. While the autoregressive component underperforms independently, combining it with diffusion yields the best results, suggesting complementary strengths. This demonstrates that our diffusion planning enhances the NLU capabilities of comparable autoregressive models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:63.5pt;vertical-align:-30.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-127.3pt,23.4pt) scale(0.575438746873729,0.575438746873729) ;">
