</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>StoryCloze Generation</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Setup.</span> To evaluate generation quality, we assess narrative continuations on the StoryCloze dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mostafazadeh et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, which requires coherent fifth-sentence completions for four-sentence stories. We employ Claude 3.7 Sonnet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>)</cite> as a judge to compare STAR-LDM against baseline models (GPT-2 Large/XL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and Pythia models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Biderman et al., 2023</a>)</cite>) in blind head-to-head evaluations across 200 contexts, using four criteria: Narrative Coherence, Commonsense Reasoning, Language Quality, and Emotional/Psychological Plausibility. Full evaluation details, including the prompt, are in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D StoryCloze Evaluation Details ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
STAR-LDM significantly outperforms similar-sized and larger models across all criteria ( <a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6 StoryCloze Generation ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 4</span></a>), with win rates exceeding 70% against models like GPT-2 XL on Coherence/Reasoning (<math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.01</annotation></semantics></math>). These substantial gains suggest our latent diffusion planning effectively improves global structure and logical consistency compared to pure autoregression. While competitive against much larger models (Pythia 6.9/12B), STAR-LDM’s improvements in Language Quality are less pronounced, indicating the planning mechanism primarily enhances semantic coherence rather than surface fluency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20528v1/figures/storycloze_winrate.png" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/220;" width="476" height="220" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S6.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">LLM-Judge evaluation. Results presented with 95% confidence intervals.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
