</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Language generation evaluation on C4 validation examples. We report mean and standard error over three sets of generations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Setup.</span> We evaluate generation on 5000 C4 validation samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffel et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, generating 64-token continuations from 32-token prefixes. We evaluate using three complementary metrics: (1) <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama Ppl</span> measures fluency using perplexity computed by Llama-3.2-3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; (2) <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MAUVE</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pillutla et al., 2021</a>)</cite> assesses distributional similarity to human-written text using GPT-2 Large embeddings; and (3) <span id="S7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Div</span> quantifies lexical diversity as <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{n=2}^{4}\frac{|\text{unique n-grams}|}{|\text{total n-grams}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext>unique n-grams</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext>total n-grams</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{n=2}^{4}\frac{|\text{unique n-grams}|}{|\text{total n-grams}|}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. For STAR-LDM, we report metrics across different classifier-free guidance strengths (<math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>), which increase the influence of the prefix, during diffusion planning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S7.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 7 Language Generation Evaluation ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, STAR-LDM consistently outperforms baselines in MAUVE score, while maintaining competitive perplexity and diversity. Increasing prefix guidance improves fluency without sacrificing diversity. We present results regarding inference latency in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Inference Latency ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Plug-and-Play Control</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Setup.</span> STAR-LDM enables attribute steering during generation without model retraining. We evaluate sentiment control and toxicity mitigation and compare against DeXperts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>—a strong baseline that fine-tunes specialized language models to guide generation. In contrast, our approach only requires training a lightweight MLP classifier on the target attribute to guide diffusion planning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For toxicity control, we trained our MLP on the Jigsaw Unintended Bias dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">cjadams et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. The toxicity guidance was evaluated using 1,000 randomly selected neutral prompts from RealToxicityPrompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gehman et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
For sentiment control, we used both Amazon Polarity <span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/amazon_polarity" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/datasets/amazon_polarity</a></span></span></span> and Stanford Sentiment Treebank (SST-2) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Socher et al., 2013</a>)</cite> to train our sentiment MLP classifier. We tested sentiment steering using 1,000 randomly selected neutral prompts from OpenWebText <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gokaslan et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For evaluation in both settings, we generated 10 continuations per prompt. We measure the alignment with the control condition, language fluency, and language diversity. For toxicity, we follow prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng and Raffel (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2021a)</a></cite>and use the Perspective API. For sentiment we utilize RoBERTa-Large<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/siebert/sentiment-roberta-large-english" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/siebert/sentiment-roberta-large-english</a></span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2020</a>)</cite> fine-tuned on sentiment classification across diverse domains.
For language fluency, we report the generative perplexity with Llama-3.2-3B. To quantify generation diversity, we follow prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2022b</a>)</cite> and measure the average number of unique 3-grams in each set of continuations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> <a href="#S8.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 8 Plug-and-Play Control ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 5</span></a> shows STAR-LDM achieves better control-fluency trade-offs than DeXperts across guidance scales for both negative sentiment and toxicity reduction. Our model generates near-zero positive sentiment text at lower perplexity than GPT-2 Large, while maintaining lexical diversity even under strong guidance (additional results presented in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Additional Control Experiments ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix E</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S8.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S8.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20528v1/ppl_vs_neg_sentiment.svg" id="S8.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/380;" width="476" height="380"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S8.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S8.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Negative Sentiment (Lower is better)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S8.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20528v1/ppl_vs_max_toxicity.svg" id="S8.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/379;" width="476" height="379"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S8.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S8.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Toxicity Mitigation (Lower is better)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S8.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S8.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Relationship between perplexity and content attributes across guidance scales (<math id="S8.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work explores both discrete diffusion (applying noise to token sequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoogeboom et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoogeboom et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>) and continuous approaches (in embedding space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strudel et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gulrajani and Hashimoto, 2023</a>)</cite> or latent spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lovelace et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lovelace et al., 2024</a>)</cite>). While discrete models lag behind autoregressive transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, continuous models show promise for controllability but face integration challenges that our unified architecture addresses.
Controlled generation methods include model fine-tuning (domain-adaptive pretraining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gururangan et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, control tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2022b</a>)</cite>, RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>) and guided decoding with lightweight components (classifier guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dathathri et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang and Klein, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng and Raffel, 2023</a>)</cite> or specialized LMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krause et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>). Our approach performs guidance during diffusion planning for semantic control.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work enhances LM capabilities through natural language reasoning (chain-of-thought prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zelikman et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zelikman et al., 2024</a>)</cite>), specialized tokens for paused thinking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, recurrent computation over hidden states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geiping et al., 2025</a>)</cite>), or by learning discrete latent actions via inverse dynamics models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jia et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Our work introduces ”thinking” through diffusion in semantic space, complementing discrete token generation.
Hybrid generative architectures have been explored in prior work primarily for multimodal settings rather than enhancing language generation alone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented STAR-LDM, a unified architecture that integrates autoregressive language modeling with latent diffusion planning. Our experiments demonstrate three key advantages: (1) improved natural language understanding capabilities, (2) higher quality narrative generation with better coherence and reasoning, and (3) effective plug-and-play control without model fine-tuning. By enabling models to ”stop and think” in a continuous semantic space before generating text, STAR-LDM combines the global planning capabilities of diffusion models with the fluency of autoregressive generation. This approach not only improves performance on standard tasks but also enables precise control of generated text attributes through lightweight guidance mechanisms. Our work represents a step toward language models that more closely mirror human writing processes, pausing to plan before committing to specific wording.</p>
</div>
</section>
