</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2 </span>Decoding Parameters</h3>

<div id="A6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all of our generation experiments, we utilize nucleus sampling (p=0.9) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holtzman et al., 2020</a>)</cite> with a repetition penalty of 1.2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Keskar et al., 2019</a>)</cite> for the STAR-LDM decoder and the autoregressive baselines.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.3 </span>Baseline NLU Results</h3>

<div id="A6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All baseline results are computed with the lighteval evaluation harness<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://github.com/huggingface/lighteval" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/huggingface/lighteval</a></span></span></span> using the FineWeb <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Penedo et al., 2024</a>)</cite> evaluation prompts<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceFW/fineweb/blob/main/lighteval_tasks.py" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceFW/fineweb/blob/main/lighteval_tasks.py</a></span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We report results for CommonsenseQA (CSQA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Talmor et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, Social IQA (SIQA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sap et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, HellaSwag (HS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zellers et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, Winogrande (WG) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sakaguchi et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, Physical IQA (PIQA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bisk et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, OpenBookQA (OBQA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mihaylov et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, and ARC (Easy and Challenge) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.4 </span>Noise-Conditioned MLP</h3>

<div id="A6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present results of the noise conditioned classifier for sentiment and toxicity detection on held-out sets for our dataset in <a href="#A6.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ F.4 Noise-Conditioned MLP ‣ Appendix F Implementation Details ‣ Stop-Think-AutoRegress: Language Modeling with Latent Diffusion Planning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 16</span></a>. We observe that they smoothly improve in performance as noise levels decrease, outperforming a logistic regression baseline for even moderately noisy data.</p>
</div>
